      "text_raw": "As an example, let us design\na CMOS symmetrical OTA\nfor a GBW of 200 MHz and\n2 pF load capacitance.\nThe expressions of the\nGBW and f are repeated.\nnd\nObviously, for wide-band\nperformance, we have to\ntake a high-speed transistor\nfor M4 and M6. This means\nthat this current amplifier\n(or mirror) devices have\nto be designed for large\nV −V and small L.\nGS T\nSome values have been\nselected, depending on the\nCMOS process available. The resulting f is about 5 GHz.\nT\nThe maximum value of B is found by equating f to 3×GBW. The value of B is therefore 5.\nnd\nMany designers use between 3 and 5.\nThe input transconductance is now easily obtained from the GBW. It is g =0.5 mS, which\nm1\nrequires about 50 mA. The total current consumption is now 0.6 mA.\nThe FOM of this amplifier is 670 MHzpF/mA, which is quite good, indeed!",
      "text": "As an example, let us design a CMOS symmetrical OTA for a GBW of 200 MHz and 2 pF load capacitance. The expressions of the GBW and f are repeated. nd Obviously, for wide-band performance, we have to take a high-speed transistor for M4 and M6. This means that this current amplifier (or mirror) devices have to be designed for large V −V and small L. GS T Some values have been selected, depending on the CMOS process available. The resulting f is about 5 GHz. T The maximum value of B is found by equating f to 3×GBW. The value of B is therefore 5. nd Many designers use between 3 and 5. The input transconductance is now easily obtained from the GBW. It is g =0.5 mS, which m1 requires about 50 mA. The total current consumption is now 0.6 mA. The FOM of this amplifier is 670 MHzpF/mA, which is quite good, indeed!"
